### This **BIG PR** is a complete rewrite of the `zenoh/commons` crates, with `no_std` and `no_alloc` in mind, and a new project structure aligned with a more minimal, protocol-focused architecture.

---

## Objective

* Implement the entire Zenoh stack inside a single crate.
* Provide one external crate per platform implementation.
* Remove all static memory pools introduced in #4 to allow **zero-copy** message transmission and reception, using only user-allocated stack buffers.

---

## New Project Structure

```text
.
├── platforms/
│   ├── zenoh-nostd-wasm/
│   └── zenoh-nostd-embassy/
└── src/
    ├── api/
    ├── io/
    ├── keyexpr/
    ├── protocol/
    ├── platform/
    └── lib.rs
```

---

## Protocol Changes

All protocol primitives that carry payloads now reference external buffers using lifetimes, e.g.:

```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
pub struct Put<'a> {
    pub timestamp: Option<Timestamp>,
    pub encoding: Encoding<'a>,
    pub ext_sinfo: Option<ext::SourceInfoType>,
    pub ext_attachment: Option<ext::AttachmentType<'a>>,
    pub payload: crate::zbuf::ZBuf<'a>,
}
```

All primitives also embed their own encoding/decoding logic:

```rust
pub fn encode(&self, writer: &mut ZBufWriter<'_>) -> ZResult<(), ZCodecError> {
    match self {
        PushBody::Put(put) => put.encode(writer),
    }
}

pub fn decode(reader: &mut ZBufReader<'a>) -> ZResult<Self, ZCodecError> {
    let header = crate::protocol::zcodec::decode_u8(reader)?;
    match imsg::mid(header) {
        id::PUT => Ok(PushBody::Put(Put::decode(header, reader)?)),
        _ => zbail!(ZCodecError::Invalid),
    }
}
```

---

## Built-in `PlatformStd`

In #4, platform support was externalized per target, but this proved cumbersome for `std` environments (e.g., Linux, macOS). This PR introduces a new `platform-std` feature for native platforms, enabling `zenoh` to run directly in `std` environments.

This makes it possible to include native examples in the `examples/` directory of the root crate.

---

## ✉️ `z_put` / `z_sub` Usage

The `z_put` function is implemented similarly to the official Rust `zenoh` API, but with explicit buffer management:

```rust
let mut session = zenoh_nostd::open!(
    PlatformStd: (spawner, PlatformStd {}),
    EndPoint::<32>::from_str("tcp/127.0.0.1:7447").unwrap()
)
.unwrap();

let ke: &'static keyexpr = "demo/example".try_into().unwrap();
let payload = b"Hello, from std!";

let mut tx_zbuf = [0u8; 64];
loop {
    session
        .put(tx_zbuf.as_mut_slice(), ke, payload)
        .await
        .unwrap();

    embassy_time::Timer::after(embassy_time::Duration::from_secs(1)).await;
}
```

> Note: The user provides the transmission buffer (`tx_zbuf`), which is used internally to serialize and send the message. The payload is copied **once** into this buffer before being sent.

---

Due to `no_alloc` constraints, a traditional `.await`-based subscriber architecture isn’t viable without copies. Instead, this PR uses an inline subscription + callback-based model:

```rust
let ke: &'static keyexpr = "demo/example".try_into().unwrap();

let mut tx_zbuf = [0u8; 256];
let subscription_1 = session
    .declare_subscription(tx_zbuf.as_mut_slice(), ke)
    .await
    .unwrap();

let mut rx_buffer = [0u8; 512];
loop {
    session
        .read(rx_buffer.as_mut_slice(), async |subscription, sample| {
            if subscription == subscription_1 {
                // `sample` is a subslice of `rx_buffer`, no copy involved
            }
        })
        .await
        .unwrap();
}
```

> 🔍 Note: The receive buffer (`rx_buffer`) is user-owned. Received messages reference this buffer directly as slices — **zero copy**, fully stack-allocated.
